{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Manawatū-Whanganui}}
'''Rangitīkei''' (before 2008 spelled '''Rangitikei''' without a macron) is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Rangitīkei is [[Suze Redmayne]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Member for Rangitīkei, National Party |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/redmayne-suze/ |website=www.parliament.nz |publisher=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=3 November 2023}}</ref> She has held this position since [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]].

The electorate has existed continuously since the [[1860–1861 New Zealand general election|1861 general election]].

==Profile==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Rangitīkei.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Rangitīkei is the third largest general electorate by area in the North Island. It encircles, but does not include, [[Palmerston North]]. The electorate straddles [[New Zealand State Highway 1|State Highway 1]] through [[Bulls, New Zealand|Bulls]], [[Marton, New Zealand|Marton]], [[Taihape]], and [[Waiouru]] as far as [[Mount Ruapehu]]. Its largest centre is [[Feilding]]. Its western boundary, from south of [[Whanganui]], extends northwards to include the communities of [[Ohakune]], [[National Park, New Zealand|National Park]], and [[Taumarunui]]. At the 2014 boundary review, the population of the RangitĪkei electorate was below tolerance and projected to decline further, so the Representation Commission shifted population around [[Shannon, New Zealand|Shannon]] from {{NZ electorate link|Ōtaki}} into RangitĪkei.<ref name="Electorate profile">{{cite web |title= Rangitīkei electorate profile|url= https://www.parliament.nz/resource/en-NZ/DBHOH_Lib_EP_Rangit%C4%ABkei_Electoral_Profile/ff28bf90b4641a8d57c1274a030a9542e96570e9 |date= June 2015 |publisher=Parliamentary Library |access-date= 7 July 2017}}{{CC-notice|cc=by3|author=the Parliamentary Library}}</ref> At the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would be shifted southwards to accommodate boundary changes in the [[Kāpiti Coast District|Kāpiti]] region, gaining the [[Horowhenua District|Horowhenua]] communities of [[Foxton, New Zealand|Foxton]], [[Levin, New Zealand|Levin]] and [[Manakau]], while no longer retaining the communities around [[Palmerston North]] and north of [[Taihape]].<ref>{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref>

Between [[2006 New Zealand census|census 2006]] and [[2013 New Zealand census|census 2013]], the Rangitīkei electorate experienced a 0.4% decline in population in comparison to a 5.3% increase in New Zealand as a whole. One in ten (10.0%) stated their highest qualification as a Level 2 certificate, the fourth-largest share among general electorates. One in ten (10.4%) also listed their occupation as a community and personal service worker, the fifth-largest percentage. Six industries accounted for close to two-thirds (61.3%) of those working in 2013: agriculture, forestry, and fishing (16.8%); manufacturing (9.3%); education and training (9.0%); public administration (8.9%); health care and social assistance (8.9%); and retail trade (8.4%).<ref name="Electorate profile" />

==History==
A seat named [[Wanganui and Rangitikei]] was contested at the very first general election in New Zealand in [[1853 New Zealand general election|1853]]. The use of an electorate named Rangitikei in its own right dates from the [[3rd New Zealand Parliament|third session]] of the New Zealand Parliament. In a somewhat auspicious start for the seat, the first Member of Parliament for the seat in [[1860–1861 New Zealand general election|1861]] was future Prime Minister [[William Fox (New Zealand politician)|William Fox]]. Fox resigned twice; first on 16 May 1865, causing the {{By-election link|Rangitikei|1865}} (won by [[Robert Pharazyn]]), and then on 11 March 1875, causing the {{By-election link|Rangitikei|1875}} (won by [[John Ballance]]).{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=107}}

Three members died while holding the seat: [[Douglas Hastings Macarthur]] died on 24 May 1892 and was succeeded by [[John Stevens (New Zealand politician)|John Stevens]]; [[Arthur Remington]] died on 17 August 1909 and was succeeded by [[Robbie Smith (politician)|Robbie Smith]]; and [[Roy Jack|Sir Roy Jack]] died on 24 December 1977 and was succeeded by [[Bruce Beetham]].

The current boundaries of the seat date from the introduction of [[mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]]. The seat was created by adding the southern tip of [[King Country (New Zealand electorate)|King Country]] to the northern tip of the [[Manawatu (New Zealand electorate)|Manawatu]] seat, and drafting in the towns to the east of [[Whanganui]] from [[Waitotara (New Zealand electorate)|Waitotara]]. The rural [[Conservatism|conservative]] nature of the seat makes it a safe National seat, though for six years in the 1970s and 80s it was held by a third party MP, [[Social Credit Party (New Zealand)|Social Credit]] leader [[Bruce Beetham]].

